1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,880
I'm back with Reiner Pope, CEO 
of MatX, a new AI chip company. 

2
00:00:04,880 --> 00:00:07,440
Last time we were talking about 
what happens inside a data center. 

3
00:00:07,440 --> 00:00:10,560
Now I want to understand what 
happens inside an AI chip. 

4
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:13,680
How does a chip actually work?
Full disclosure, by the way:  

5
00:00:13,680 --> 00:00:19,440
I am an angel investor in MatX.
So hopefully you have designed a good chip. 

6
00:00:19,440 --> 00:00:25,840
Hope so. I'll start with the smallest 
fundamental unit of chip design, and we'll  

7
00:00:25,840 --> 00:00:30,880
build up to what an actual production 
chip is and what its components are. 

8
00:00:30,880 --> 00:00:36,480
At the very bottom level of a chip, the 
primitives we work with are logic gates,  

9
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:42,640
very simple things like AND, OR, and NOT.
These are connected together by wires that have to  

10
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:50,080
be laid out physically as metal traces on a chip.
The main function that AI chips want to compute  

11
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:54,720
is the multiplication of matrices.
Inside that, the fundamental primitive  

12
00:00:54,720 --> 00:01:01,680
is a multiply-accumulate of pairs of numbers.
We're going to demonstrate what that calculation  

13
00:01:01,680 --> 00:01:07,280
looks like by hand, and then infer what 
a circuit would look like for that. 

14
00:01:08,080 --> 00:01:17,360
It'll be easiest if I do a multiply-accumulate of 
a four-bit number with another four-bit number. 

15
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:24,080
The clearest primitive is 
actually multiply-accumulate. 

16
00:01:24,080 --> 00:01:40,000
So there's a multiply of these two terms, and 
then we're going to add in an eight-bit number. 

17
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:47,840
Can I ask a clarifying question?
Why is this the natural primitive for  

18
00:01:49,280 --> 00:01:53,600
whatever computation happens inside a computer?
There are a few reasons. 

19
00:01:55,280 --> 00:02:00,960
It's a little bit more efficient, but the 
reason it's natural for AI chips is that if  

20
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:06,400
you look at what's happening during a matrix 
multiply… What is a matrix multiply in short? 

21
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:21,520
There's a for-loop over i, over 
j, and over k, of output [i,  

22
00:02:21,520 --> 00:02:36,080
k] += input [i, j] x other input [j, k].
A multiply-accumulate happens at every  

23
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:40,640
single step of a matrix multiply.
The other observation is that the  

24
00:02:40,640 --> 00:02:45,520
precision will almost always be higher in the 
accumulation step than in the multiplication step. 

25
00:02:45,520 --> 00:02:49,840
This is specific to AI chips.
You're multiplying low-precision numbers,  

26
00:02:49,840 --> 00:02:54,240
and then when you accumulate, errors accumulate 
quickly, so you need more precision there. 

27
00:02:54,240 --> 00:02:58,240
This is why we've chosen to do a four-bit 
multiplication and an eight-bit addition. 

28
00:02:58,240 --> 00:03:01,440
Let me make sure I understood that.
There are two ways to understand that. 

29
00:03:01,440 --> 00:03:06,000
One is that the value will 
be larger than the inputs. 

30
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:10,800
The other is that if it was a 
floating-point number it would be…  

31
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:14,160
Maybe that part is less intuitive to me.
But maybe it's the same principle? 

32
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:20,400
It really is the same principle.
The separate principle is that as  

33
00:03:20,400 --> 00:03:23,680
you're summing up this number, you're summing 
up a whole bunch of numbers, so you've got a  

34
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:28,000
lot of rounding errors accumulating.
Whereas in this case, there's only  

35
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,560
one multiplication in the chain, 
so there aren't a lot of rounding  

36
00:03:30,560 --> 00:03:33,920
errors accumulating in the multiplication.
Why are you summing up a whole bunch of numbers? 

37
00:03:33,920 --> 00:03:39,280
There’s just two numbers there.
This summation is repeated j many times. 

38
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:44,480
Any errors accumulate. I see.
So how would we perform this calculation by hand? 

39
00:03:45,040 --> 00:03:48,240
As a human, we would probably 
separate it into two, but we can do  

40
00:03:48,240 --> 00:03:55,760
it all in one using long multiplication.
For the multiplication term first, we're  

41
00:03:55,760 --> 00:04:03,120
going to multiply this four-bit number by every 
single bit position in the other four-bit number. 

42
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:08,720
We write that out. First, 1001 
multiplied by this bit position. 

43
00:04:10,240 --> 00:04:14,800
That is the number itself.
Then shifted across by one,  

44
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:20,720
we're multiplying by 0.
That gives us an all-0 number. 

45
00:04:20,720 --> 00:04:28,800
Shifted across one more to 
multiply by this one, we get 1001. 

46
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:33,440
Finally, for this last bit position, 
we get an all-0 number again. 

47
00:04:37,120 --> 00:04:42,960
This gives us a bunch of terms that 
we have to add for the multiplication. 

48
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:50,160
While we're doing that summation, we might as 
well add in the actual accumulator term as well. 

49
00:04:50,160 --> 00:05:00,000
So we just copy that directly across.
So this is the sum. 

50
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:02,720
It's a five-way sum that we want to compute. 

51
00:05:07,520 --> 00:05:12,160
What logic gates did it take us 
to get to this intermediate step? 

52
00:05:12,160 --> 00:05:16,960
We needed to produce all 16 
of these partial products. 

53
00:05:17,920 --> 00:05:23,920
How do I produce one of these partial products?
Let's take this number 1, for example here. 

54
00:05:26,080 --> 00:05:30,720
We produce it by multiplying this 
number by this one over here. 

55
00:05:31,440 --> 00:05:35,600
We can produce that with an AND gate.
This number is 1 if both this bit  

56
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:37,920
is 1 and this bit is 1.
If either of them is 0,  

57
00:05:37,920 --> 00:05:45,600
then the multiplication of 0 times anything is 0.
To produce all of this, we ended up  

58
00:05:45,600 --> 00:05:50,880
consuming 16 AND gates.
In the general case,  

59
00:05:50,880 --> 00:06:06,560
if I were doing a p bit multiply times a q bit 
multiply, this will be p times q many ANDs. 

60
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:15,040
Finally, I sum them. Most of the work 
is going to happen in the summing. 

61
00:06:15,680 --> 00:06:20,480
Let me describe the other 
logic gate that we use here. 

62
00:06:20,480 --> 00:06:23,200
AND is almost the simplest logic 
gate that exists on a chip. 

63
00:06:23,200 --> 00:06:30,480
It's almost the smallest. At the other extreme, 
the very largest logic gate you'll typically use  

64
00:06:30,480 --> 00:06:39,040
is something called a full adder.
Coming from software, you might think  

65
00:06:39,040 --> 00:06:43,680
that a full adder adds 32-bit numbers together.
In this case, it just adds three single-bit  

66
00:06:43,680 --> 00:06:48,320
numbers together, so you can think 
of it as adding 0, 1, and 1 together. 

67
00:06:50,400 --> 00:06:54,320
When I add these together, the result can 
be 0, 1, 2, or 3, so I can express that  

68
00:06:54,320 --> 00:06:58,880
in binary using just two bits.
As input, it has three bits. 

69
00:06:58,880 --> 00:07:04,960
As output, it has two bits.
The number 2 in binary is 10. 

70
00:07:06,880 --> 00:07:17,600
This is also known as a 3→2 compressor 
because it takes three bits of input  

71
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:19,520
and produces two bits of output.
Just to make sure I understood: the  

72
00:07:19,520 --> 00:07:25,200
two inputs are an X and a Y value 
and then some carry that came in… 

73
00:07:27,120 --> 00:07:34,320
The three inputs are all bits in the same bit 
position, like three bits in a column here. 

74
00:07:35,120 --> 00:07:41,360
The two outputs, I've drawn them vertically 
here and horizontally here to match this  

75
00:07:41,360 --> 00:07:45,920
vertical versus horizontal layout.
This expresses that things in the same  

76
00:07:45,920 --> 00:07:50,160
column are in the same bit position, whereas 
things in adjacent columns are different. 

77
00:07:50,160 --> 00:07:56,800
This is a carry out, whereas this was the sum.
So if the inputs in the full adder were, say,  

78
00:07:56,800 --> 00:07:59,280
101, then the output would be 10.
If it were 111, it'd be 11. 

79
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:06,800
If it were 000, it'd be 00.
If it were 010, it'd still be 01. 

80
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:09,440
Got it.
Yeah. It's essentially just counting the  

81
00:08:09,440 --> 00:08:17,120
number of things and expressing that in binary.
This circuit captures what we as humans naturally  

82
00:08:17,120 --> 00:08:24,960
do when we're summing along a column.
I'll show one iteration of using  

83
00:08:24,960 --> 00:08:27,840
the full adder to sum.
The way I sum here is going  

84
00:08:27,840 --> 00:08:31,760
to be a little unnatural for humans.
We would sum along the column and then  

85
00:08:31,760 --> 00:08:38,160
remember the carry, but instead of remembering 
the carry, we'll explicitly write it out. 

86
00:08:39,120 --> 00:08:42,160
We proceed from the rightmost 
column toward the left. 

87
00:08:42,160 --> 00:08:51,360
On the rightmost column, we sum the 1 and the 1, 
and that produces a zero here and a carry of one. 

88
00:08:51,360 --> 00:08:59,200
We've used this full adder circuit on this pair 
of bits and produced a pair of bits as output. 

89
00:08:59,200 --> 00:09:04,880
Now we can do the same thing with this column.
We have a column of four numbers, so we'll take  

90
00:09:04,880 --> 00:09:11,520
the first three of them, run a full adder on 
them, and that gives us a 0 and a 0 as output. 

91
00:09:12,320 --> 00:09:18,720
The sum of these is 00.
That's the full adder applied to all these bits. 

92
00:09:18,720 --> 00:09:25,840
As I've used up bits, I'll cross them 
out to indicate that I've handled them. 

93
00:09:25,840 --> 00:09:31,200
Let's keep going a little bit more.
I take these three numbers, I add  

94
00:09:31,200 --> 00:09:36,880
them, and that gives me a 1 and a 0.
I've dealt with these three numbers. 

95
00:09:36,880 --> 00:09:44,160
Now I take these three numbers and add 
them, and that gives me a 1 and a 0,  

96
00:09:44,160 --> 00:09:51,840
and I've dealt with these numbers.
The way to view this is that I have  

97
00:09:51,840 --> 00:09:57,120
this whole grid of numbers that need to be added.
I'm going to keep applying full adders to all the  

98
00:09:57,120 --> 00:10:03,600
bits here, constantly removing three numbers from 
a column and writing out two numbers as output. 

99
00:10:03,600 --> 00:10:09,200
Keep going over and over again until I 
eventually get just one single number coming out. 

100
00:10:16,640 --> 00:10:30,800
This approach is called a Dadda multiplier.
This is the standard for how you do  

101
00:10:31,520 --> 00:10:38,400
area-efficient multipliers using full adders.
Let's try to quantify the circuit size of  

102
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:43,440
this so we have a sense of how big 
things are and can compare them later. 

103
00:10:44,720 --> 00:10:49,360
How many full adders did I use?
How many numbers did I start with? 

104
00:10:50,080 --> 00:10:57,200
I have the 16 partial products, which is a product 
of all of these terms with all of these terms,  

105
00:10:57,200 --> 00:11:06,480
plus the eight terms that I'm adding here.
I started off with 24 bits. 

106
00:11:06,480 --> 00:11:14,480
Eventually I produced eight bits on the output.
In every step, I was crossing off three numbers  

107
00:11:14,480 --> 00:11:20,080
and writing two numbers out as a result.
Every single use of a full adder eliminates  

108
00:11:20,080 --> 00:11:23,840
one of the bits here.
So how many full adders? 

109
00:11:23,840 --> 00:11:28,800
It must be 24 minus 8, so there 
were 16 full adders in this circuit. 

110
00:11:30,000 --> 00:11:34,000
This is true in the general case as well.
There will be p times q many  

111
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:38,400
full adders in this circuit.
Let me make sure I understand the logic of that. 

112
00:11:39,520 --> 00:11:42,407
The input bits, 24, is p x q, plus p + q.
The output bits are just p + q. 

113
00:11:42,407 --> 00:12:00,080
So p x q plus p + q, minus p + q equals p x q.
That's right. I think this explains,  

114
00:12:00,720 --> 00:12:06,400
or at least hints at, the second reason 
we chose to do a multiply-accumulate. 

115
00:12:06,400 --> 00:12:10,960
The first reason is that it's what 
shows up in matrix multiplication. 

116
00:12:10,960 --> 00:12:16,080
The second is that it gave us this very 
slick, simple p x q, very simple algebra. 

117
00:12:17,600 --> 00:12:22,480
We've described this whole procedure.
Every single atomic step that I took  

118
00:12:22,480 --> 00:12:26,640
here becomes a logic gate, and then 
the wires are connected together. 

119
00:12:26,640 --> 00:12:31,840
When I had these three inputs that I used to 
produce these two outputs, if I think of mapping  

120
00:12:31,840 --> 00:12:38,560
this to a physical device, there would be a wire 
connecting all three of these things together  

121
00:12:38,560 --> 00:12:46,720
into a logic gate that produced this output.
This is the main primitive, at different bit  

122
00:12:46,720 --> 00:12:54,000
widths, that's inside an AI chip.
We're going to build up from here  

123
00:12:54,000 --> 00:12:58,000
to how you would use it to run all 
the other operations you might want. 

124
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:02,800
This might be the wrong time to ask, but 
whenever Nvidia reports that this chip  

125
00:13:02,800 --> 00:13:11,120
can do X many FP4 or half as many FP8, it 
seems to imply those circuits are fungible,  

126
00:13:11,680 --> 00:13:17,760
that there's not a dedicated FP4 versus FP8.
But the way you're mapping it out here, it seems  

127
00:13:17,760 --> 00:13:27,440
like if it has to be mapped out in the logic, you 
would need a dedicated FP4 multiply-accumulate and  

128
00:13:27,440 --> 00:13:33,040
then a dedicated FP8 accumulate.
Can you "funge" them? 

129
00:13:33,040 --> 00:13:37,920
As drawn, they're not particularly fungible.
This is actually one of the main choices you  

130
00:13:37,920 --> 00:13:42,560
have to make when designing a chip: how 
much of FP4 and how much of FP8 do I have? 

131
00:13:42,560 --> 00:13:47,760
Sometimes I'll make that consideration from 
the point of view of the customer requirement. 

132
00:13:47,760 --> 00:13:55,280
Another angle is to equalize the 
power budget between FP4 and FP8. 

133
00:13:55,280 --> 00:14:02,000
When they report those numbers and it just happens 
to be the case that it does 2x as many FP4 as FP8,  

134
00:14:02,000 --> 00:14:08,320
they're just choosing to give equivalent 
die areas to all the floating points,  

135
00:14:08,320 --> 00:14:12,160
and as a result it ends up being…?
Why is the ratio exactly 2x? 

136
00:14:13,680 --> 00:14:18,480
Part of it is that surely it won't 
be exactly equivalent to die area. 

137
00:14:20,400 --> 00:14:23,520
There's a data movement reason.
We'll maybe come back to this when we  

138
00:14:24,080 --> 00:14:28,800
look at how it goes into and out of memories.
There's something really nice from a software  

139
00:14:28,800 --> 00:14:32,080
level about the fact that I can pack 
two four-bit numbers into the same  

140
00:14:32,080 --> 00:14:34,640
storage as an eight-bit number.
When I store that to memory,  

141
00:14:36,960 --> 00:14:44,960
the sizing of the buses that I wire out within 
the chip makes that work out really nicely. 

142
00:14:44,960 --> 00:14:52,160
Come to think of it, it's not just 2x.
The amount of area it takes sounds like  

143
00:14:54,160 --> 00:15:00,240
it's quadratic with the bit length.
That's why smaller precision is even  

144
00:15:00,240 --> 00:15:02,960
more favorable than you might think.
This is a really big reason. 

145
00:15:04,160 --> 00:15:10,640
In fact, Nvidia made a change.
Historically, up until B100 or  

146
00:15:10,640 --> 00:15:16,400
B200, every time you halved the bit 
precision, you doubled the FLOP count. 

147
00:15:18,160 --> 00:15:21,680
For the reason you said, because of this quadratic 
scaling, that ratio is actually slightly wrong. 

148
00:15:21,680 --> 00:15:26,480
You should get an even bigger speedup 
than you might otherwise think. 

149
00:15:28,080 --> 00:15:34,960
Nvidia’s product specs have started acknowledging 
that in B300 and beyond, where the FP4 is three  

150
00:15:34,960 --> 00:15:38,560
times faster than the FP8.
Though it should be 4x. 

151
00:15:38,560 --> 00:15:42,160
Yeah. What I've shown here is the 
simplest case of integer multiply. 

152
00:15:42,880 --> 00:15:48,480
When you're dealing with floating point, as 
you do in FP4 and FP8, there's this other term,  

153
00:15:49,520 --> 00:15:54,880
the exponent, that complicates the calculation.
What can we see already from this? 

154
00:15:54,880 --> 00:15:58,560
I think the big observation you've made is that 
there's this quadratic scaling with bit width,  

155
00:16:00,000 --> 00:16:04,640
which is very effective and is 
the single reason low-precision  

156
00:16:04,640 --> 00:16:11,280
arithmetic has worked so well for neural nets.
The other thing we're going to do now is compare  

157
00:16:11,280 --> 00:16:17,360
the area spent on the multiplication 
itself with all the circuitry around it. 

158
00:16:20,800 --> 00:16:27,760
We'll walk back in time a little bit and 
see how GPUs prior to Tensor Cores worked,  

159
00:16:28,320 --> 00:16:34,560
which is in fact the same way CPUs worked.
Where do we stick this multiply-accumulate unit? 

160
00:16:37,120 --> 00:16:47,840
Generically, I'll describe a CUDA core or a CPU.
You'll have some register file which stores some  

161
00:16:47,840 --> 00:16:58,480
number of entries, maybe eight entries of, in this 
case, 4-bit numbers, but typically 32-bit numbers. 

162
00:17:03,440 --> 00:17:06,800
Inside the CUDA core, I'll have 
some register file of some depth,  

163
00:17:06,800 --> 00:17:18,000
and then I'll have my multiply-accumulate circuit.
What it's going to do is take three arbitrary  

164
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:21,280
registers from this register file, 
perform the multiply-accumulate,  

165
00:17:21,280 --> 00:17:25,920
and then write back to the register file.
It's going to write to this one,  

166
00:17:25,920 --> 00:17:31,440
but it was able to read from this 
one, this one, and another random one. 

167
00:17:31,440 --> 00:17:39,920
It will take three inputs like this.
This is the core data path of many processors. 

168
00:17:39,920 --> 00:17:42,640
Most processors look like this.
You've got some set of registers, and  

169
00:17:42,640 --> 00:17:50,800
then you've got some set of logic units, or ALUs.
We want to analyze the cost of the data movement  

170
00:17:50,800 --> 00:17:58,320
from the register file to the ALU and back.
Ultimately, there's going to be some  

171
00:17:58,320 --> 00:18:01,040
circuit that says, "Well, I don't 
always have to select this guy. 

172
00:18:01,040 --> 00:18:04,480
I might select any of the 
registers at any point in time." 

173
00:18:04,480 --> 00:18:11,920
The first question is: how can I build a circuit?
The circuit I'm going to look for is a mux. 

174
00:18:13,040 --> 00:18:17,680
In this case, it's going to have eight inputs, 
one from each entry of the register file,  

175
00:18:17,680 --> 00:18:25,120
and it's going to have one output, 
which is actually producing this output. 

176
00:18:26,080 --> 00:18:32,960
What is the cost of this thing?
All we have to build it out of is AND and OR. 

177
00:18:32,960 --> 00:18:37,680
How do we build it?
We do the dumbest thing possible. 

178
00:18:37,680 --> 00:18:44,640
We form a mask. When we want to read the 
third entry, we're going to AND every  

179
00:18:44,640 --> 00:18:48,000
single entry with either 1 or 0 based 
on whether that's what we want to read,  

180
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:52,240
and then we're going to OR all of them together.
Just to make sure I understand the basics. 

181
00:18:52,240 --> 00:18:59,360
What the mux is doing is just selecting an input?
Just selecting, invisible to software. 

182
00:18:59,360 --> 00:19:02,240
You say "I want input number three," 
and that means there's a mux here. 

183
00:19:02,240 --> 00:19:06,080
So what is the cost of this mux?
An n-input  

184
00:19:10,640 --> 00:19:21,120
mux operating on p bits.
I have n rows. That's just eight  

185
00:19:21,120 --> 00:19:25,600
rows, and each row is p bits wide.
I have to AND every single bit,  

186
00:19:25,600 --> 00:19:32,320
so I get n x p many AND gates.
For every single input I have to decide  

187
00:19:32,320 --> 00:19:37,680
whether I'm going to mask it out or not.
Then I'm going to OR them all together. 

188
00:19:38,960 --> 00:19:47,040
There's going to be n – 1 times p many OR gates.
I've got all of these different things,  

189
00:19:47,040 --> 00:19:53,600
almost all of them are 0s, but I need to collapse 
them from my eight options down into one option. 

190
00:19:54,640 --> 00:19:58,560
Every step, I need to OR one 
row into an existing row. 

191
00:19:59,440 --> 00:20:04,240
It's actually funny that you don't 
think at the level of hardware. 

192
00:20:04,240 --> 00:20:09,760
You just think, "Oh, I'll just select element 
three," and something as simple as that is  

193
00:20:11,440 --> 00:20:15,280
in and of itself quite a complicated circuit.
This is the first step of all of the hidden  

194
00:20:15,280 --> 00:20:22,480
data movement costs that show up.
We're just going to compare. 

195
00:20:23,200 --> 00:20:26,080
I have to pay this cost.
I've got one mux here, and in fact  

196
00:20:26,080 --> 00:20:30,960
I have two more copies of that for each of the 
three inputs to my multiply-accumulate operation. 

197
00:20:30,960 --> 00:20:42,640
I have this cost, which is 3 x n x p AND gates 
over here, compared to p x q gates in the actual  

198
00:20:42,640 --> 00:20:48,640
circuit that is doing the thing I care about.
If we plug in actual numbers, with n being  

199
00:20:49,760 --> 00:21:00,560
eight, I get 24 x p gates just in the data 
movement, compared to—if q is four—4 x p  

200
00:21:00,560 --> 00:21:05,520
gates just in the multiply-adder.
Where is the three coming from? 

201
00:21:05,520 --> 00:21:14,720
Three different inputs here. What I'm 
hinting at is that all of this work,  

202
00:21:14,720 --> 00:21:18,640
which scales as the size of the register 
file—and this is a very small register  

203
00:21:18,640 --> 00:21:26,080
file—all of this work just moving the data from 
the register file to the logic unit is many,  

204
00:21:26,080 --> 00:21:31,040
many times more expensive than the logic unit.
In the most recent ClusterMAX report, SemiAnalysis  

205
00:21:31,040 --> 00:21:36,480
ranked almost 100 different GPU clouds. Crusoe 
was one of only five that made the gold tier. 

206
00:21:36,480 --> 00:21:41,120
SemiAnalysis found that gold-tier providers 
like Crusoe had a total cost of ownership that  

207
00:21:41,120 --> 00:21:46,160
was 5% to 15% lower than silver-tier ones, 
even when they had identical GPU pricing. 

208
00:21:46,160 --> 00:21:50,480
This makes sense because total cost of ownership 
is downstream of a bunch of different things that  

209
00:21:50,480 --> 00:21:54,480
don't necessarily show up in the sticker 
price, but that Crusoe has optimized. 

210
00:21:54,480 --> 00:21:58,400
Things like how well you detect faults 
and how quickly you replace failed nodes. 

211
00:21:58,400 --> 00:22:04,080
For example, Crusoe was one of the first clouds 
to adopt NVSentinel, NVIDIA's own GPU monitoring  

212
00:22:04,080 --> 00:22:09,440
and self-healing software for enhanced 
GPU uptime, utilization, and reliability. 

213
00:22:09,440 --> 00:22:14,240
This lets Crusoe make use of everything that 
NVIDIA has learned about why chips fail across all  

214
00:22:14,240 --> 00:22:18,880
their different fleets and deployments, so that 
Crusoe can catch faults earlier in the process. 

215
00:22:18,880 --> 00:22:23,760
And once they identify a failure, Crusoe can 
swap in a healthy node in less than 10 minutes. 

216
00:22:23,760 --> 00:22:27,200
Because they’re not running bare metal, Crusoe 
doesn't have to spend time installing an  

217
00:22:27,200 --> 00:22:31,120
operating system or configuring drivers.
They can just spin up a new VM on an  

218
00:22:31,120 --> 00:22:34,800
already running and pre-qualified host.
If you want to learn more about this or  

219
00:22:34,800 --> 00:22:41,840
the other reasons that Crusoe made 
gold tier, go to crusoe.ai/dwarkesh. 

220
00:22:41,840 --> 00:22:44,480
It may be helpful to just 
see what a mux looks like,  

221
00:22:44,480 --> 00:22:53,600
maybe a two-bit or a four-bit mux.
We’ll do a two-way mux. 

222
00:22:53,600 --> 00:23:00,560
We've got two different numbers, 
we’ve got these two inputs. 

223
00:23:03,120 --> 00:23:16,240
These are the inputs that are being selected 
between, and the selector can either be "I want  

224
00:23:16,240 --> 00:23:24,080
this one" or "I want the other one."
This is a one-hot encoding. 

225
00:23:24,800 --> 00:23:34,160
This is what we start with.
Let's focus on this case. 

226
00:23:34,160 --> 00:23:40,480
This is the actual input we got, and we 
want to produce this guy as the result. 

227
00:23:42,560 --> 00:23:47,840
Very laboriously, we AND 
this bit with all of these. 

228
00:23:50,240 --> 00:23:55,200
That produces ANDing this bit with this row.
Likewise, we AND this bit with this row. 

229
00:23:55,200 --> 00:24:07,360
That produces all 0s. There are four ANDs here.
Finally,  

230
00:24:07,360 --> 00:24:09,440
we OR these two together, and this gives a 1.
We OR these two together, this gives a 1. 

231
00:24:09,440 --> 00:24:15,120
We OR these two together, it gives a 0.
We OR these two together and it gives a 1. 

232
00:24:15,680 --> 00:24:22,560
Those are the four ORs.
This actually ends up  

233
00:24:22,560 --> 00:24:27,280
looking a little bit like addition.
We did exactly the same set of ANDs. 

234
00:24:28,640 --> 00:24:34,080
We've ANDed all of these things together, 
but then instead of collapsing it by using  

235
00:24:34,080 --> 00:24:38,560
full adder circuits, we just get a 
very simple collapsing with OR gates. 

236
00:24:38,560 --> 00:24:46,480
But that doesn't look like n times p.
This was with n=2 inputs. 

237
00:24:48,320 --> 00:25:03,920
In the general case, we will have n 
rows, and we'll have p bits per row. 

238
00:25:03,920 --> 00:25:11,680
That gives us the n times p many AND gates.
In this circuit I've described, almost all of  

239
00:25:11,680 --> 00:25:19,440
the cost, seven-eighths of the cost, is in reading 
and writing the register file, and only a tiny  

240
00:25:19,440 --> 00:25:23,280
fraction of the cost is in the logic unit itself.
This is the problem to solve. 

241
00:25:24,720 --> 00:25:31,760
This essentially was the state of play prior 
to the Volta generation of Nvidia GPUs. 

242
00:25:31,760 --> 00:25:36,720
This kind of thing is what 
was inside the CUDA cores. 

243
00:25:37,360 --> 00:25:41,760
This problem statement is what motivated 
the introduction of Tensor Cores,  

244
00:25:41,760 --> 00:25:46,240
which are more generically called systolic arrays.
Think about how we're going to solve this problem. 

245
00:25:46,240 --> 00:25:48,480
We're spending almost all of our 
circuit area on something that we  

246
00:25:48,480 --> 00:25:52,080
really don't care about and is hidden to the 
software programmer, and the thing that we  

247
00:25:52,080 --> 00:25:57,520
actually care about is not much of the area.
Make this one bigger somehow while keeping  

248
00:25:57,520 --> 00:25:59,440
this at the same size.
That's the goal. 

249
00:26:01,360 --> 00:26:06,320
The evolution was that we had baked 
this much into hardware at this stage. 

250
00:26:06,320 --> 00:26:11,280
This single line is a multiply-accumulate, 
and this single thing was baked into hardware. 

251
00:26:11,840 --> 00:26:17,920
The idea of a systolic array is to go 
two levels of loops up and bake this  

252
00:26:17,920 --> 00:26:22,480
entire loop out here into hardware.
The idea is that if we have a much  

253
00:26:22,480 --> 00:26:27,280
bigger granularity fixed-function 
piece of logic, maybe the taxes we  

254
00:26:27,280 --> 00:26:30,400
pay on the input and output are much smaller.
Interesting. It sounds like you're suggesting  

255
00:26:30,400 --> 00:26:41,520
that if you go up one step in the matrix 
multiply loop, you can tilt the balance  

256
00:26:41,520 --> 00:26:46,080
more towards compute than communication.
That's right. There are two effects we're  

257
00:26:46,080 --> 00:26:49,280
going to take advantage of here.
One is that we can do more stuff  

258
00:26:49,840 --> 00:26:58,640
per every trip through our register file.
The other is that in some of this loop, we can  

259
00:26:58,640 --> 00:27:07,040
take advantage of certain things staying fixed.
Visually, we're going to look at this  

260
00:27:07,040 --> 00:27:09,840
matrix multiplication.
This portion of the loop  

261
00:27:09,840 --> 00:27:20,240
corresponds to a matrix-vector multiplication.
We'll take a matrix and multiply it by a vector. 

262
00:27:21,600 --> 00:27:23,760
How do we do this?
Every column gets  

263
00:27:23,760 --> 00:27:27,840
multiplied by the vector and then summed.
We're going to sum along columns. 

264
00:27:28,560 --> 00:27:32,960
This 0 and 3 gets multiplied by the 3 and 
7 and gets summed, and then the 1 and 2  

265
00:27:32,960 --> 00:27:38,880
gets multiplied by the 3 and 7 and gets summed.
There is a multiply-accumulate associated with  

266
00:27:38,880 --> 00:27:47,200
every single one of these entries in the matrix.
We'll draw out these four multiply-accumulates. 

267
00:27:53,840 --> 00:28:01,600
Just to make sure I understand why there are 
four multiply-accumulates: each entry in the  

268
00:28:01,600 --> 00:28:09,360
column that corresponds to the output 
vector is a dot product, and in this  

269
00:28:09,360 --> 00:28:16,240
case it will be two multiplications and then 
the addition of those two multiplications. 

270
00:28:16,240 --> 00:28:20,240
You're accumulating...
Really there's only one addition per  

271
00:28:20,240 --> 00:28:23,760
dot product, but we like to start with zero.
But it includes the initialization of zero. 

272
00:28:23,760 --> 00:28:30,240
Yeah. We want to have quadratically more compute. 

273
00:28:30,240 --> 00:28:39,120
We have x times y as much 
compute as we had before. 

274
00:28:41,360 --> 00:28:50,800
But we want to aim for having only 
x times as much communication. 

275
00:28:52,000 --> 00:28:57,600
The intention is to get this 
advantage term going as y. 

276
00:28:57,600 --> 00:29:06,080
We've laid down the multiplications.
We want to bring in a vector of size two, and  

277
00:29:06,080 --> 00:29:11,920
that is already in line with our columns target.
That's fine. However, we need to manage the  

278
00:29:11,920 --> 00:29:16,640
communication of this matrix, 
which exceeds our budget of x. 

279
00:29:18,480 --> 00:29:26,080
The idea is that in an AI context, this matrix 
is going to stay fixed for a long period of time. 

280
00:29:29,760 --> 00:29:36,480
We've got some register files sitting over here.
The amount of stuff coming out of this register  

281
00:29:36,480 --> 00:29:41,920
file… this is the term that we 
want to go as x, in some sense. 

282
00:29:43,920 --> 00:29:47,360
We don't want to bring this full matrix 
in from the register file every cycle,  

283
00:29:47,360 --> 00:29:52,320
because that would cost too much in 
terms of wiring from the register file. 

284
00:29:55,200 --> 00:30:00,720
Our key trick is that this matrix can 
be stored locally to the systolic array. 

285
00:30:04,080 --> 00:30:15,280
We'll store these numbers 0, 1, 2, and 3 
in a gate called a register that physically  

286
00:30:15,280 --> 00:30:19,040
stores these numbers, and we're going to 
reuse these numbers over and over again  

287
00:30:19,040 --> 00:30:23,840
for a large number of different vectors.
The optimization here is that the nature of  

288
00:30:23,840 --> 00:30:35,280
matrix multiplication is that you can store this 
square quadratic thing directly where the logic is  

289
00:30:35,280 --> 00:30:45,520
happening, which has an extra dimension compared 
to the inputs that you keep swapping in and out. 

290
00:30:45,520 --> 00:30:46,800
That’s right.
This is the nature of  

291
00:30:46,800 --> 00:30:52,080
what a matrix multiplication is.
You do a lot of multiplication  

292
00:30:52,080 --> 00:30:55,280
to get one value out.
A dot product is the  

293
00:30:55,280 --> 00:30:59,760
result of a lot of multiplications.
So that optimization means you can  

294
00:30:59,760 --> 00:31:03,040
stuff a lot of multiplication in 
before you get some value out of it. 

295
00:31:03,040 --> 00:31:07,280
That's right. Just to complete the 
picture of concretely how that looks:  

296
00:31:09,360 --> 00:31:17,440
I swapped the 3 and the 2 here.
Just like this 0 and 3 is going  

297
00:31:17,440 --> 00:31:25,120
to multiply by the 3 and the 7, we're going 
to form a dot product along columns here. 

298
00:31:26,480 --> 00:31:35,200
We're going to feed a 3 and a 7 in here.
This feeds into this multiplication and also  

299
00:31:35,200 --> 00:31:39,360
feeds into this multiplication.
Likewise, the 3 feeds  

300
00:31:39,360 --> 00:31:48,400
into here and also into here.
Then we're going to sum along here. 

301
00:31:49,120 --> 00:31:53,680
Starting at the top of a column, we feed in 0s, 
and then coming out the bottom we get results. 

302
00:31:55,840 --> 00:32:03,120
Visually, there's a dot product performed along 
columns in a matrix, and that maps exactly to  

303
00:32:03,120 --> 00:32:09,040
what is done spatially in the systolic array.
This is one dot product summed vertically,  

304
00:32:09,040 --> 00:32:12,560
and this is a second dot 
product also summed vertically. 

305
00:32:13,600 --> 00:32:17,040
What is the data that needs to go 
into and out of the register file? 

306
00:32:17,040 --> 00:32:23,680
We have x amount of data coming 
out on the output, and we also  

307
00:32:23,680 --> 00:32:33,840
have x amount of data coming from the input.
With respect to the input and output vectors  

308
00:32:33,840 --> 00:32:44,720
at least, we've met our goal of having only x as 
much data going in and out of the register file. 

309
00:32:44,720 --> 00:32:51,120
This leaves open the question: I said the 
weight matrix is stored locally in the systolic  

310
00:32:51,120 --> 00:32:56,800
array, so how did it get there in the first place?
At some point, you need to boot your chip and  

311
00:32:56,800 --> 00:33:02,240
populate this data, so where did that come from?
The trick is that we just do it very slowly. 

312
00:33:02,240 --> 00:33:05,760
We very slowly trickle-feed 
it into the systolic array. 

313
00:33:07,200 --> 00:33:13,280
The simplest strategy is that we run 
this daisy chain: feed a number in here,  

314
00:33:13,280 --> 00:33:18,000
and on the next clock cycle it will move 
down to the next entry of the systolic array. 

315
00:33:18,000 --> 00:33:26,320
We can do that in every column in parallel, 
this is also going to come from here,  

316
00:33:26,320 --> 00:33:32,160
and that gives us another factor of 
approximately x units of bandwidth coming in. 

317
00:33:32,720 --> 00:33:34,960
Would you mind repeating 
that sentence one more time? 

318
00:33:36,960 --> 00:33:40,720
We know that we're going to be bringing 
numbers only rarely into the matrix. 

319
00:33:43,200 --> 00:33:47,520
We just want to come up with any construction 
at all such that the amount of wiring that  

320
00:33:48,960 --> 00:33:56,720
crosses the boundary of the systolic 
array is bounded to x and not go as xy. 

321
00:33:56,720 --> 00:34:03,440
A particularly simple strategy is that 
we bring a number into the top row of  

322
00:34:03,440 --> 00:34:11,840
the systolic array in one clock cycle.
Then, for y consecutive clock cycles,  

323
00:34:11,840 --> 00:34:16,800
we bring in the top row every time and 
shift all the other rows down by one. 

324
00:34:16,800 --> 00:34:22,080
This keeps the wiring that needs to 
come from this expensive register file  

325
00:34:22,080 --> 00:34:27,360
only down to a factor of x rather than xy.
I see. There are two questions in terms  

326
00:34:27,360 --> 00:34:33,280
of communication: communication 
time and communication bandwidth. 

327
00:34:33,840 --> 00:34:39,280
You're saying that since we're only going to be 
loading this in once, let's minimize bandwidth,  

328
00:34:39,280 --> 00:34:45,760
because bandwidth equals die area.
We load it in slowly over smaller  

329
00:34:45,760 --> 00:34:48,640
lanes because we're just going to 
keep this value in there for a while. 

330
00:34:48,640 --> 00:34:52,160
Exactly.
It's interesting to me that when we were talking  

331
00:34:52,160 --> 00:34:58,240
last time about inference across many chips, the 
big high-level thing we're trying to optimize for  

332
00:34:58,240 --> 00:35:03,760
is increasing the amount of compute per memory 
bandwidth, that is to say, per communication. 

333
00:35:03,760 --> 00:35:08,640
Here also, we're trying to increase 
the amount of actual multiplies  

334
00:35:08,640 --> 00:35:16,400
or additions relative to transporting 
information from registers to the logic. 

335
00:35:16,400 --> 00:35:19,680
In both cases, you're trying to maximize 
compute relative to communication. 

336
00:35:20,480 --> 00:35:27,840
This shows up all the way up and down the stack.
This is close to the bottom, to the gates. 

337
00:35:28,400 --> 00:35:34,640
There's a version that's maybe even closer 
to the gates in the precision of the number  

338
00:35:34,640 --> 00:35:39,040
format that you choose to use.
We saw that same effect. 

339
00:35:39,040 --> 00:35:47,280
There's a squared versus linear term going 
on both purely in the precision of the ALU,  

340
00:35:47,280 --> 00:35:55,440
but also in the size of the matrix.
This unit is the next bigger unit. 

341
00:35:55,440 --> 00:36:01,840
We had the multiplication circuit, and on top 
of that we have a pretty large systolic array. 

342
00:36:01,840 --> 00:36:11,040
I drew it as 2x2, but older TPUs were described 
as 128x128 of this circuit shown here. 

343
00:36:12,240 --> 00:36:20,400
This ends up being the most efficient known 
circuit for implementing a matrix multiply. 

344
00:36:21,360 --> 00:36:26,080
We've talked about how it seems 
obvious that you should try to  

345
00:36:26,080 --> 00:36:31,120
maximize compute relative to communication.
What are non-obvious trade-offs that  

346
00:36:35,040 --> 00:36:39,520
keep you up at night, about whether you should do 
X or Y and it's not obvious what the answer is? 

347
00:36:41,360 --> 00:36:46,800
Most of the decisions in chip 
design are sizing decisions. 

348
00:36:48,640 --> 00:36:56,560
Already in what we've drawn so far… AI 
chips all have this circuit in them. 

349
00:36:56,560 --> 00:37:02,240
They have a systolic array, and somewhere near 
it a register file providing inputs and outputs. 

350
00:37:06,400 --> 00:37:11,120
Even within this scope, the sizing questions you 
have are: how big should I make my systolic array,  

351
00:37:11,120 --> 00:37:23,200
and how big should I make the register file?
These two questions are coupled. 

352
00:37:25,280 --> 00:37:32,800
One way to think of it is to set a 
budget for what percentage of chip  

353
00:37:32,800 --> 00:37:36,960
area you want to spend on data movement.
Maybe I say that I want this to be 10%  

354
00:37:36,960 --> 00:37:42,640
and the systolic array to be 90%.
Then I can size my register file. 

355
00:37:42,640 --> 00:37:47,520
Bigger register files are more flexible.
They allow me to get more application-level  

356
00:37:47,520 --> 00:37:54,160
performance out, but they take away from 
the area spent on the systolic array. 

357
00:37:55,040 --> 00:37:59,520
I recently ran an essay contest where I asked 
people to write about what I consider to be some  

358
00:37:59,520 --> 00:38:03,040
of the biggest open questions about AI.
The submission window closed last week,  

359
00:38:03,040 --> 00:38:07,680
so I used Cursor to create a couple of different 
interfaces to help me review the entries. 

360
00:38:07,680 --> 00:38:10,800
One interface anonymizes submissions 
and hides the necessary information. 

361
