371
00:38:50,480 --> 00:38:54,720
and help me understand it and work with me on it.
So whatever you're working on,  

372
00:38:54,720 --> 00:39:00,720
you should do it in Cursor.
Go to cursor.com/dwarkesh. 

373
00:39:00,720 --> 00:39:08,000
Where does the clock cycle of a chip come in?
What determines what that is? 

374
00:39:08,000 --> 00:39:12,480
And what is a clock cycle of a chip?
At baseline, it's worth observing  

375
00:39:12,480 --> 00:39:16,880
that chips are incredibly parallel.
You've got 100 billion transistors in a chip. 

376
00:39:16,880 --> 00:39:21,200
A key thing you need to do whenever you 
have massive parallelism is synchronize  

377
00:39:21,200 --> 00:39:27,360
between the different parallel units.
In software, typically you have these very  

378
00:39:27,360 --> 00:39:31,520
expensive synchronization methods like a mutex.
One thread will finish what it's doing,  

379
00:39:34,560 --> 00:39:38,640
grab a lock stored somewhere in memory, 
and notify the other thread that it's done. 

380
00:39:39,600 --> 00:39:50,080
On chips, we take a very different approach.
Every nanosecond or so, all circuitry in the chip  

381
00:39:50,080 --> 00:39:57,360
will pause for a moment and synchronize.
That is the clock cycle. 

382
00:39:58,240 --> 00:40:06,000
The entire chip typically goes in lockstep 
to the next operation in one fell swoop. 

383
00:40:06,000 --> 00:40:17,840
What this looks like in circuitry is that 
the clock is mediated by registers, which  

384
00:40:17,840 --> 00:40:23,840
are these storage devices we've drawn elsewhere.
The way to think of it is: I have some storage  

385
00:40:24,400 --> 00:40:29,280
holding a bit, which might be 0 or 1.
Then I have some cloud of logic,  

386
00:40:29,280 --> 00:40:42,480
which maybe is this systolic array or multiplier.
I have a bunch of inputs feeding into this cloud  

387
00:40:42,480 --> 00:40:48,640
of logic, and eventually there's going to 
be some output register that it writes to. 

388
00:40:48,640 --> 00:40:55,040
There is a global clock signal 
driving all these registers. 

389
00:40:57,120 --> 00:41:05,120
At a certain instance in time, when the clock 
strikes, whatever value happens to be on that  

390
00:41:05,120 --> 00:41:15,280
wire at that instant is what gets stored.
The challenge is that I would like to have  

391
00:41:15,280 --> 00:41:19,680
my clock speed run as fast as possible.
If I run at two gigahertz, I get twice as  

392
00:41:19,680 --> 00:41:23,760
many operations done per second 
as if I run at one gigahertz. 

393
00:41:24,960 --> 00:41:30,400
But what that ends up meaning is that I'm very 
sensitive to the delay through this cloud of  

394
00:41:30,400 --> 00:41:37,280
logic, because any computation happening in there 
needs to finish before the next clock cycle hits. 

395
00:41:40,880 --> 00:41:53,200
A major point of optimization on any chip 
is to make this delay as short as possible. 

396
00:41:53,200 --> 00:42:02,800
Interesting. The constraint here seems 
to be that if you add too much logic,  

397
00:42:02,800 --> 00:42:07,760
you might risk missing the clock cycle.
But if you don't add enough, you're  

398
00:42:07,760 --> 00:42:15,840
leaving potential compute on the table.
Is there ever a situation where you take  

399
00:42:15,840 --> 00:42:23,440
a probabilistic chance that a computation 
finishes, or is it strictly that it either  

400
00:42:23,440 --> 00:42:28,720
finishes by the clock cycle or it doesn't?
In standard chip design, you margin it such  

401
00:42:28,720 --> 00:42:34,640
that there is a probability, but 
it's many standard deviations out. 

402
00:42:34,640 --> 00:42:38,880
For all intents and purposes, it is a 
reliable part and will always meet the clock. 

403
00:42:40,720 --> 00:42:45,760
There are some weird exceptions, like clock domain 
crossings where you go from one clock to another. 

404
00:42:45,760 --> 00:42:47,600
Then you actually do have to 
reason about this probability. 

405
00:42:47,600 --> 00:42:55,760
But in the main path, you margin it such that 
you'll get there 25% of the clock cycle in  

406
00:42:55,760 --> 00:43:07,520
advance, making it very unlikely that it misses.
Where the clocks synchronize, where the  

407
00:43:07,520 --> 00:43:11,360
registers are, is this something 
you determine as a chip designer? 

408
00:43:11,360 --> 00:43:18,960
Or is it an artifact where you want a certain 
sequence of logic, and the software you use to  

409
00:43:18,960 --> 00:43:25,120
convert your Verilog into what you send to 
TSMC just determines that to make it work,  

410
00:43:25,120 --> 00:43:33,440
you have to put a register here, here, and 
here, making sure no single step makes the  

411
00:43:33,440 --> 00:43:38,240
whole chip's clock cycle longer than it has to be?
Inserting them is actually a huge part of the  

412
00:43:38,240 --> 00:43:42,560
work of designing a chip.
It's done by a combination  

413
00:43:42,560 --> 00:43:48,960
of manual and automatic methods.
To show the very dumb version of  

414
00:43:48,960 --> 00:43:52,080
what you can do here, you can take 
this logic and split it in half. 

415
00:43:53,680 --> 00:44:02,400
Instead of just one cloud of logic, I can have two 
smaller clouds of logic that do the same thing,  

416
00:44:02,400 --> 00:44:13,120
but split them up by a register.
If you split it in the middle,  

417
00:44:13,120 --> 00:44:17,120
you can hit twice the clock frequency.
That's great, you get twice the performance,  

418
00:44:17,120 --> 00:44:22,080
but at the cost of an extra 
register, which means more storage. 

419
00:44:22,080 --> 00:44:24,560
Stepping back, why do we need 
to synchronize the whole chip? 

420
00:44:25,360 --> 00:44:28,949
If you imagine playing Factorio or 
something, there's no global clock cycle. 

421
00:44:28,949 --> 00:44:31,760
Things are just done when they're done.
There's iron on the plate, and you can  

422
00:44:31,760 --> 00:44:36,640
take it if you want.
Taking that analogy,  

423
00:44:36,640 --> 00:44:41,840
the thing you need to be mindful of is if I 
have two different paths through some logic. 

424
00:44:41,840 --> 00:44:47,520
Say I have to do computation 
f here and computation g here,  

425
00:44:47,520 --> 00:44:58,400
and they're going to meet for computation h.
There's going to be manufacturing variance. 

426
00:44:59,440 --> 00:45:03,920
In some chips f will take a little longer; 
in some chips g will take a little longer. 

427
00:45:03,920 --> 00:45:10,640
If I have a signal propagating through, and 
the results from f and g have to meet up at h,  

428
00:45:12,400 --> 00:45:16,640
what can go wrong is that f gets 
there early and it meets the previous  

429
00:45:16,640 --> 00:45:20,640
value of g, or the next value of g.
Ah. And h needs to know when to start,  

430
00:45:21,440 --> 00:45:24,320
when the next iteration has…
Exactly. 

431
00:45:24,320 --> 00:45:30,240
This explains why different chips made 
at the same process node, the same TSMC  

432
00:45:32,080 --> 00:45:38,480
technology, can have different clock cycles.
Two chips made at 3 nm might have different clock  

433
00:45:38,480 --> 00:45:47,520
cycles based on whether they were able to optimize 
to ensure no single critical path is so long that  

434
00:45:48,800 --> 00:45:53,600
it slows down the whole chip's clock cycle.
That's right. This optimization I showed here  

435
00:45:53,600 --> 00:45:59,040
is called pipeline register insertion.
We've inserted a register in the  

436
00:45:59,040 --> 00:46:02,400
middle of the pipeline.
This is a pure trade-off  

437
00:46:02,400 --> 00:46:08,000
between clock speed and area.
That is the easy case. 

438
00:46:08,000 --> 00:46:14,320
There is a harder case too.
I drew out a pipeline of logic,  

439
00:46:14,320 --> 00:46:21,360
