As a logic designer, in principle,  

465
00:48:57,840 --> 00:49:07,200
if you just had a register and an AND gate in a 
loop, you could get an insanely fast clock speed,  

466
00:49:07,200 --> 00:49:16,720
more than four, five, or six gigahertz.
But if you take this really simple circuit  

467
00:49:16,720 --> 00:49:23,920
and look at the area you're spending 
here… This is called one gate equivalent  

468
00:49:23,920 --> 00:49:28,400
in size, so unit of one in area.
This thing is maybe a unit of eight in area. 

469
00:49:31,520 --> 00:49:34,400
Again, almost all your cost 
becomes synchronization or  

470
00:49:34,400 --> 00:49:40,800
communication cost compared to the actual logic.
This would be a case where you've gone too far. 

471
00:49:40,800 --> 00:49:43,840
You've made your clock speed really 
fast at the cost of spending almost  

472
00:49:43,840 --> 00:49:50,480
all of your area on pipeline registers.
Interesting. So you're hinting at a dynamic  

473
00:49:50,480 --> 00:49:54,720
where you can have a really fast clock speed 
but you're not getting that much work done. 

474
00:49:56,480 --> 00:50:04,560
You can have low latency but low throughput.
It hurts your throughput, in fact, because  

475
00:50:05,760 --> 00:50:11,680
