Suppose in the first mux you're pulling  

576
01:00:45,200 --> 01:00:52,080
from eight nearby registers as input.
That's a total of 32 bits going in. 

577
01:00:53,440 --> 01:00:58,640
Out of that, four bits come out.
Those four bits go into the second mux,  

578
01:00:58,640 --> 01:01:02,720
which is inside the lookup table.
In this case, these registers  

579
01:01:02,720 --> 01:01:06,400
are single-bit registers.
If there are eight nearby  

580
01:01:06,400 --> 01:01:11,440
registers and lookup tables, then I 
have eight bits total coming in nearby. 

581
01:01:11,440 --> 01:01:21,760
I select from eight down to four different values.
There are actually four different muxes, a little  

582
01:01:21,760 --> 01:01:26,400
mux associated with each of these input bits.
Each of them is selecting one out of eight. 

583
01:01:26,400 --> 01:01:30,400
Where are those eight coming from?
Nearby registers and other LUTs. 

584
01:01:30,400 --> 01:01:32,320
Each register is one bit.
Yes. 

585
01:01:33,360 --> 01:01:40,720
I guess AMD or whoever makes these FPGAs 
still has to be opinionated about which  

586
01:01:42,000 --> 01:01:47,680
registers are connected to which registers.
You can program in the actual gates,  

587
01:01:47,680 --> 01:01:52,080
but they add a wire in the connect… 
the communication topology, right? 

588
01:01:53,760 --> 01:02:00,000
You get flexibility at a local grain.
There's a nearby neighborhood you can select from,  

589
01:02:00,000 --> 01:02:06,160
but for more coarse, long-distance 
connections, they form an opinion on that. 

590
01:02:06,160 --> 01:02:11,360
And the reason it's 10x slower is why?
If you look at the cost of building this  

591
01:02:11,360 --> 01:02:17,120
lookup table, it's 32 gates.
It can give me the equivalent  

592
01:02:17,120 --> 01:02:21,280
of—what’s an interesting thing I 
can do here—a four-way AND gate. 

593
01:02:26,960 --> 01:02:35,440
A four-way AND means AND, AND, 
and then an AND of an AND. 

594
01:02:36,400 --> 01:02:40,720
This is a circuit I could implement in 
an ASIC directly using three AND gates. 

595
01:02:41,520 --> 01:02:46,640
Using a LUT, I can also implement it, but 
it's going to take 32 gates instead of three. 

596
01:02:47,200 --> 01:02:58,560
So the overhead is really coming from 
the fact that there's a more concise  

597
01:02:58,560 --> 01:03:05,440
way to describe a truth table than listing out 
every single possible combination of inputs,  

598
01:03:06,400 --> 01:03:11,360
which is just to write out the gate.
Yes, to place down the polysilicon  

599
01:03:11,360 --> 01:03:15,120
and the wires and so on.
Interesting. One important point you  

600
01:03:15,120 --> 01:03:21,600
made to me is that the reason they prefer FPGAs to 
CPUs is that they get deterministic clock cycles. 

601
01:03:21,600 --> 01:03:28,720
They know when a packet will come in and go out.
Why isn't that a guarantee in CPUs? 

602
01:03:28,720 --> 01:03:32,400
You can actually design a CPU that 
has deterministic latency as well. 

603
01:03:34,640 --> 01:03:40,480
In fact, the processors inside a lot of 
AI chips also have deterministic latency. 

604
01:03:40,480 --> 01:03:43,120
Groq has advertised this. TPUs 
have that in the core as well. 

605
01:03:46,080 --> 01:03:50,400
The challenge is getting deterministic 
latency and high speed at the same time. 

606
01:03:53,280 --> 01:04:01,520
Non-deterministic latency comes from 
specific design choices in a CPU. 

607
01:04:03,520 --> 01:04:08,640
It's actually possible to remove those design 
choices and make a CPU with deterministic latency,  

608
01:04:08,640 --> 01:04:13,760
but those are not very attractive in the 
market, so people don't make those CPUs anymore. 

609
01:04:16,960 --> 01:04:24,960
In some sense, deterministic latency is a simpler 
starting point, and some chip designers have  

610
01:04:24,960 --> 01:04:33,440
added things in to make it non-deterministic.
To take a concrete example, probably the most  

611
01:04:33,440 --> 01:04:39,280
important source of non-determinism 
on a CPU is the CPU cache itself. 

612
01:04:43,920 --> 01:04:50,640
In a CPU, you have the CPU die itself, 
and then DDR memory off on the side. 

613
01:04:54,400 --> 01:05:07,120
You have a cache system inside that remembers 
recent accesses to DDR and stores them. 

614
01:05:09,200 --> 01:05:11,600
When I'm running through my 
CPU instructions, every time  

615
01:05:12,240 --> 01:05:17,600
I have an instruction that accesses memory, it 
first checks if the data was stored in the cache. 

616
01:05:17,600 --> 01:05:23,840
If not, it fetches it from DDR.
This is a huge optimization. 

617
01:05:23,840 --> 01:05:27,200
The cache is two orders of 
magnitude faster than the DDR. 

618
01:05:27,200 --> 01:05:33,600
If you never used the cache, basically all 
programs would run a hundred times slower. 

619
01:05:34,160 --> 01:05:38,240
The presence of a cache is absolutely necessary 
for a CPU to run at a reasonable speed. 

620
01:05:39,360 --> 01:05:45,760
But whether or not you get a cache hit 
depends on the ambient environment of the CPU:  

621
01:05:45,760 --> 01:05:48,880
what other programs are running, what has 
run recently, and what the random number  

622
01:05:48,880 --> 01:05:53,200
generator inside the cache system is doing.
That is a big source of non-determinism  

623
01:05:53,200 --> 01:05:58,800
in the runtime of a CPU.
That is the memory system for a CPU. 

624
01:06:01,600 --> 01:06:06,320
The big thing you can do differently 
is, instead of having the hardware say,  

625
01:06:06,320 --> 01:06:10,720
"I’m going to read memory" and then the 
hardware decides whether or not it comes  

626
01:06:11,280 --> 01:06:18,560
from the cache, you can bake this decision 
into software, a different design philosophy. 

627
01:06:22,480 --> 01:06:28,640
You see this in TPUs, for example.
I'll draw the same diagram,  

628
01:06:28,640 --> 01:06:36,240
but I'll call it a scratchpad.
The main difference is… This would be a TPU,  

629
01:06:36,240 --> 01:06:41,680
and you have HBM in this case rather than 
DDR, but it's still an off-chip memory. 

630
01:06:43,280 --> 01:06:48,480
Instead of the software saying "first access 
memory" and letting the hardware decide,  

631
01:06:48,480 --> 01:06:53,280
you have one kind of instruction that goes to 
the scratchpad and a totally different kind  

632
01:06:53,280 --> 01:06:58,160
of instruction that goes to HBM.
This style is generically known  

633
01:06:58,160 --> 01:07:04,160
as scratchpad instead of cache.
The key distinction is that you have  

634
01:07:04,160 --> 01:07:08,480
one kind of instruction that says "read or write 
scratchpad," and a totally different instruction  

635
01:07:08,480 --> 01:07:12,880
that says "read or write HBM."
So scratchpad being the cache. 

636
01:07:12,880 --> 01:07:20,000
Yeah, this thing here is the scratchpad.
Stepping way back: people say computers have the  

637
01:07:22,320 --> 01:07:28,240
"von Neumann architecture", where there's 
this serial processing of information. 

638
01:07:28,240 --> 01:07:30,400
Maybe it's just because we've 
been talking about parallel  

639
01:07:30,400 --> 01:07:40,720
accelerators, but the FPGA is super parallel.
The AI accelerators, the TPUs, are super parallel. 

640
01:07:41,280 --> 01:07:44,960
Even CPUs are super parallel if you 
think about all the cores they have. 

641
01:07:46,800 --> 01:07:50,080
In what sense is modern hardware 
actually the von Neumann architecture? 

642
01:07:50,080 --> 01:07:52,560
Is that actually a fair way 
to describe modern hardware? 

643
01:07:52,560 --> 01:07:58,480
I think it's a fair way to describe CPUs.
The amount of parallelism you get on a CPU is  

644
01:07:58,480 --> 01:08:05,680
about 100 cores times maybe 16-way vector 
units, so about 1,000-way parallelism on a CPU. 

645
01:08:06,800 --> 01:08:15,280
One question: there is a die being used for 
the CPU, and if there are fewer threads,  

646
01:08:15,280 --> 01:08:22,400
just as a matter of transistor voltages 
switching on and off, is it that there's  

647
01:08:22,400 --> 01:08:26,240
literally one control flow—a small part of the 
die—where voltages are switching on and off? 

648
01:08:28,240 --> 01:08:35,600
How do you actually occupy the die area of a CPU…
If there are so few cores, what are  

649
01:08:35,600 --> 01:08:37,360
you spending all of the die on?
Yeah, what is happening there? 

650
01:08:37,360 --> 01:08:39,840
The cores are just much 
bigger and more complicated. 

651
01:08:44,240 --> 01:08:50,640
We should compare a CPU core, which takes 
up one one-hundredth of the die, to a LUT. 

652
01:08:50,640 --> 01:08:54,720
A LUT is only 16 gates.
It's clear why there are so many  

653
01:08:54,720 --> 01:09:04,320
more LUTs in an FPGA than cores in a CPU.
But why are there more CUDA cores,  

654
01:09:04,320 --> 01:09:10,000
for example, than CPU cores?
What's the difference between a CPU and a GPU? 

655
01:09:13,600 --> 01:09:22,720
Inside the CPU, one big use 
of the area is the cache. 

656
01:09:23,760 --> 01:09:28,240
Very little is actually the ALUs.
Mostly it's these register  

657
01:09:28,240 --> 01:09:36,320
files rather than the logic units.
Both of those have equivalents in a GPU,  

658
01:09:36,320 --> 01:09:39,040
so that's not a big difference.
But the thing that does not have  

659
01:09:39,040 --> 01:09:47,200
an equivalent in a GPU is the branch predictor.
There is a whole big area in the CPU which is  

660
01:09:49,120 --> 01:09:58,320
just a bunch of predictors saying when the next 
branch will be and where the branch target is. 

661
01:09:58,320 --> 01:10:03,680
Stripping a lot of that out, as well 
as making these register files tighter,  

662
01:10:06,400 --> 01:10:10,160
drives a lot of the GPU gains over the CPU.
What is the purpose of the branch predictor? 

663
01:10:10,160 --> 01:10:13,440
To execute both branches at 
once, or what does it do? 

664
01:10:13,440 --> 01:10:22,000
The issue is that when I've got a series 
of instructions, if I have a branch,  

665
01:10:27,920 --> 01:10:32,880
the actual step of processing an 
instruction takes a really long time. 

666
01:10:32,880 --> 01:10:39,840
It takes maybe five nanoseconds.
The time to notice that I've got a branch,  

667
01:10:43,760 --> 01:10:49,920
evaluate whether the Boolean is true, update 
the program counter to the new target,  

668
01:10:49,920 --> 01:10:56,960
and then read from the instruction memory 
could take five nanoseconds to finish. 

669
01:10:56,960 --> 01:10:59,680
So in reality, this may 
finish somewhere down here. 

670
01:11:03,600 --> 01:11:07,120
I want to run a clock speed that is much 
faster than what five nanoseconds allows. 

671
01:11:07,920 --> 01:11:12,240
Five nanoseconds is a 200 MHz clock speed.
I would like to run at one or two gigahertz. 

672
01:11:13,200 --> 01:11:20,240
So I need to run other instructions 
while the branch is being evaluated. 

673
01:11:21,360 --> 01:11:26,880
I just want to keep running the 
instructions that happen after me. 

674
01:11:27,680 --> 01:11:30,480
But that might have been wrong.
If the branch ended up being taken, then  

675
01:11:30,480 --> 01:11:34,560
I need to know that instead of evaluating these 
instructions, I actually need to jump to wherever  

676
01:11:34,560 --> 01:11:42,080
the target is and run those instructions instead.
The purpose of the branch predictor is to predict,  

677
01:11:42,080 --> 01:11:48,720
five cycles earlier, that a branch is going to 
happen, before you even get to that instruction. 

678
01:11:49,520 --> 01:11:52,480
If I think about how the brain works 
versus what you're describing here,  

679
01:11:52,480 --> 01:11:59,760
at a high level the differences might be that 
while you can do structured sparsity in these  

680
01:11:59,760 --> 01:12:07,840
accelerators and save yourself some area that you 
would have otherwise had to dedicate to gates,  

681
01:12:07,840 --> 01:12:12,800
in the brain there's unstructured sparsity.
Any neuron can connect to any other neuron, and  

682
01:12:12,800 --> 01:12:18,800
not in ways where they have the column aligned.
Then there's the fact that memory  

683
01:12:18,800 --> 01:12:22,000
and compute are co-located.
Although I guess you could say in a way the memory  

684
01:12:22,000 --> 01:12:25,360
and compute are co-located on these dies too.
This is exactly the co-location,  

685
01:12:25,360 --> 01:12:30,400
in some sense, of the memory and compute.
So maybe that isn't a big difference. 

686
01:12:30,400 --> 01:12:40,720
Another big difference is that the clock cycle 
on the brain is much slower than on computers. 

687
01:12:40,720 --> 01:12:47,600
Partly that's to preserve energy, because the 
faster the clock cycle, the bigger the voltage  

688
01:12:47,600 --> 01:12:54,880
needs to be in order for the signal to settle 
and to identify what state a transistor is in. 

689
01:12:54,880 --> 01:12:56,160
That’s right.
I don't know if  

690
01:12:56,160 --> 01:13:05,440
you have any commentary on what the brain 
might be doing versus how these chips work. 

691
01:13:06,720 --> 01:13:16,560
Let's take the clock speed one first.
The clock speed is quite high on a  

692
01:13:16,560 --> 01:13:26,000
chip because that drives higher throughput.
When we compare a GPU running some workload,  

693
01:13:26,000 --> 01:13:29,440
it's running batch size 1,000.
Whereas the brain is not running  

694
01:13:29,440 --> 01:13:35,760
batch size 1,000, there's only one of me.
You could imagine saying, "Take a GPU and  

695
01:13:35,760 --> 01:13:39,360
instead of running at a gigahertz, run 
it at a megahertz," and that would start  

696
01:13:39,360 --> 01:13:45,600
to look a little more like the equivalent 
things you're talking about in the brain. 

697
01:13:45,600 --> 01:13:56,720
But in the way silicon works, that does not give 
you a 1,000x advantage in energy efficiency. 

698
01:13:58,480 --> 01:14:07,840
What it ends up looking like is you just 
run this circuit once to stabilization,  

699
01:14:07,840 --> 01:14:10,720
and then it will sit idle 
for a long period of time. 

700
01:14:10,720 --> 01:14:12,720
It doesn't consume a lot of 
energy while it's sitting  

701
01:14:12,720 --> 01:14:18,720
idle because most of the energy is consumed 
in toggling bits from zero to one and back. 

702
01:14:22,640 --> 01:14:26,560
Let's talk about the energy 
consumption of a circuit like this. 

703
01:14:26,560 --> 01:14:32,560
The way to think of a bit being stored is that 
you've deposited some charge in a capacitor  

704
01:14:32,560 --> 01:14:38,640
sitting somewhere in the chip implicitly.
It becomes charged when the bit becomes a one,  

705
01:14:38,640 --> 01:14:41,920
and then it becomes discharged 
when it next goes to a zero. 

706
01:14:41,920 --> 01:14:45,120
That cycle of charging the capacitor 
and then dumping that charge out  

707
01:14:45,120 --> 01:14:49,920
to ground is where the energy is consumed.
This is called the dynamic or switching power,  

708
01:14:49,920 --> 01:14:54,160
and it's most of the energy consumption of a chip.
There is some other energy consumption just  

709
01:14:54,160 --> 01:15:00,880
coming from the fact that insulators 
aren't perfect, but we'll discard that. 

710
01:15:00,880 --> 01:15:07,120
Most of the energy consumption comes from 
toggling from zero to one and back to zero. 

711
01:15:08,640 --> 01:15:12,080
If you run a chip much slower and you only 
clock it once every thousand clock cycles,  

712
01:15:12,720 --> 01:15:16,240
you will have 1,000 times fewer transitions.
It will be about 1,000 times  

713
01:15:16,240 --> 01:15:20,960
less energy consumption.
But it's not a substantial  

714
01:15:20,960 --> 01:15:25,040
advantage in energy efficiency.
Okay, so you described how a TPU  

715
01:15:25,040 --> 01:15:29,040
works at a high level.
What is the difference  

716
01:15:29,040 --> 01:15:37,200
at a high level between how a GPU and a TPU work?
There is a high-level organization principle that  

717
01:15:37,200 --> 01:15:41,920
is different, and then inside 
the cores things are different. 

718
01:15:41,920 --> 01:15:49,680
Looking at the high level, we'll 
take a GPU and a TPU and see what  

719
01:15:50,320 --> 01:15:55,040
the top-level block structure looks like.
If you think of this as the whole chip  

720
01:15:55,040 --> 01:16:03,200
in each case, the organization 
of the GPU is mostly a bunch of  

721
01:16:03,200 --> 01:16:12,080
almost-identical units, which are the SMs.
They've got an L2 memory in the middle,  

722
01:16:12,080 --> 01:16:21,520
and then a bunch more of these SMs on the bottom.
So there is a fairly regular grid of cores. 

723
01:16:24,000 --> 01:16:33,600
If we look at a TPU in comparison, you end 
up with much coarser-grained units of logic. 

724
01:16:34,160 --> 01:16:48,400
You end up with just a few matrix units, 
which are the big systolic arrays. 

725
01:16:48,400 --> 01:16:55,920
In the middle you've got some vector unit, and 
then you've got your matrix units at the bottom. 

726
01:17:00,640 --> 01:17:06,240
These matrix units with a vector unit in 
the middle make up the whole TPU chip. 

727
01:17:06,240 --> 01:17:10,480
You can think of scaling this thing 
down into a really tiny unit with a  

728
01:17:10,480 --> 01:17:15,760
smaller matrix unit and a smaller vector 
unit, and that is sort of what an SM is. 

729
01:17:16,400 --> 01:17:24,400
From a very high-level point of view, the GPU has 
a lot of tiny TPUs tiled across the whole chip. 

730
01:17:24,400 --> 01:17:31,200
Oh, interesting. You're suggesting the tensor 
core within a streaming SM is analogous to an MXU? 

731
01:17:31,200 --> 01:17:38,480
Yeah, it's all very similar.
I see. If you had more lack of structure,  

732
01:17:38,480 --> 01:17:44,080
having a bunch of tiny TPUs makes a lot of sense.
Whereas if you just have huge matrix  

733
01:17:44,080 --> 01:17:54,400
multiplications, you might want to avoid the 
cost of having individual SMs with their own  

734
01:17:54,400 --> 01:17:58,400
registers and warp schedulers.
Why not just make a huge thing  

735
01:17:58,400 --> 01:18:04,320
and amortize those costs across the whole thing?
This shows up in how large you can grow things. 

736
01:18:04,320 --> 01:18:08,240
We've seen this theme, especially with the 
systolic array, where a larger systolic  

737
01:18:08,240 --> 01:18:14,480
array amortizes the register file costs better.
This design allows you to have larger systolic  

738
01:18:14,480 --> 01:18:20,400
arrays, whereas the GPU design constrains 
you to having small units of everything. 

739
01:18:21,600 --> 01:18:29,520
There is a trade-off, however.
Because of this coarse-grained  

740
01:18:29,520 --> 01:18:34,400
separation of things, you need to move a lot of 
data from the vector unit to the matrix units,  

741
01:18:37,200 --> 01:18:46,320
through just two lines of perimeter here.
If you look at the equivalent thing  

742
01:18:46,320 --> 01:18:50,480
in a GPU, you've got vector units everywhere, and 
you can move data through many different lines. 

743
01:18:55,520 --> 01:19:00,240
The amount of data you can move between a 
vector unit and a matrix unit is actually  

744
01:19:00,240 --> 01:19:08,960
much higher in a GPU than in a TPU.
Instead of having to move all the data  

745
01:19:08,960 --> 01:19:16,720
through just two lines, you're moving 
it through 16 lines of wiring in a GPU. 

746
01:19:16,720 --> 01:19:21,200
Right. But also you might 
have to move across less area. 

747
01:19:21,200 --> 01:19:27,920
Which is an energy saving as well.
So if you can operate entirely within  

748
01:19:27,920 --> 01:19:33,760
an SM, the data movement is much smaller.
But the moment you want to operate across SMs,  

749
01:19:33,760 --> 01:19:37,760
it becomes more complicated and expensive.
So you don’t have to comment, but one might  

750
01:19:37,760 --> 01:19:45,040
expect that a thing MatX might try to do 
is get the GPU-like smaller structure of  

751
01:19:45,760 --> 01:19:55,840
systolic arrays surrounded by SRAM, but at the 
same time make it so that the things you need in  

752
01:19:55,840 --> 01:20:03,200
an SM to support the CUDA architecture—which 
take a bunch of space—you might discard. 

753
01:20:05,280 --> 01:20:09,440
We've talked publicly about something we 
call a splittable systolic array, which  

754
01:20:09,440 --> 01:20:13,600
in some sense you can think of as big systolic 
arrays that can be small systolic arrays too. 

755
01:20:13,600 --> 01:20:16,640
Cool. Okay, I think that's 
a good note to close on. 

756
01:20:16,640 --> 01:20:18,960
Reiner, thank you so much.
Thanks, Dwarkesh.
